{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Pray, etc., and remove, etc.</b> The meaning of <b>הִתְפַּלֵּל</b> is to pray for forgiveness of the sin. Now, the forgiveness of the sin changes from before the agent of harm goes forth to after it has gone forth, for then, even though the sin has been forgiven, merit is needed to remove the harmful agent — as they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Shabbat 32a), and this is their language: 'A person should always seek mercy before he falls ill, for if he falls ill they say to him: Bring merit and be released.' Therefore the generation of knowledge devised a strategy and said, corresponding to the fact that merit is needed for their deliverance from the serpents, 'and remove' — meaning that he should pray also to HaShem in the merit of Moshe to remove, etc." "<b>et the serpent.</b> They intended to say that he should remove even the minor harm, which is the bite that kills. And if they had said 'et the saraph' or 'the serpent, the saraph,' it would have been understood to refer to a particular serpent that burns the soul — that is what they are insistent about; but if one dies in the way of the world they do not ask of Him. A hermeneutic derivation: <b>אֶת הַנָּחָשׁ</b> — even the particular serpent, which is the punishment intended corresponding to how they spoke against Moshe. Also, they were precise to use the singular form, so that not even a single serpent should remain, for one can kill many and many."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:47.801929535Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}